($bc22551$life-time-plans-01$bc22551$,$bc22552$large_gap_split$bc22552$,$bc22553$Case dismissed. Security gets a baseline, and connection keeps room to breathe.$bc22553$,$bc22554$[A] = wants quality time more frequently and is dissatisfied; [B] = wants it less frequently and is currently content.$bc22554$,$bc22555$[A] experiences regular quality time as ongoing proof that the relationship still has an active place in the week. When those moments are scarce, [A] may feel lonely before [B] realizes anything changed. [B] trusts the bond across longer gaps and may hear repeated requests as pressure to perform closeness on demand. You need a dependable base rhythm plus optional extras. The base gives [A] security, while the optional layer lets [B] say yes with real energy instead of obligation.$bc22555$,$bc22556$Name the minimum rhythm that would help you feel secure without asking [B] to match every spontaneous bid. Commit to the agreed base rhythm and initiate one of those moments yourself before [A] has to request it.$bc22556$,$bc22557$Case dismissed. Security gets a baseline, and connection keeps room to breathe.$bc22557$,$bc22558$Approved v4 template$bc22558$,$bc22559$Shared Discovery$bc22559$,$bc22560$[A] = wants quality time more frequently and is dissatisfied; [B] = wants it less frequently and is currently content.$bc22560$,$bc22561$Name the minimum rhythm that would help you feel secure without asking [B] to match every spontaneous bid.$bc22561$,$bc22562$Commit to the agreed base rhythm and initiate one of those moments yourself before [A] has to request it.$bc22562$,$bc22563$Both of you save a two-week Quality Time Agreement with the minimum rhythm, optional invitations, individual downtime, and a review date.$bc22563$,$bc22564$Case dismissed. Security gets a baseline, and connection keeps room to breathe.$bc22564$),
($bc22565$life-time-plans-01$bc22565$,$bc22566$large_gap_both_discontent$bc22566$,$bc22567$Case dismissed. Predictable closeness takes pressure off every invitation.$bc22567$,$bc22568$[A] = wants quality time more frequently; [B] = wants it less frequently.$bc22568$,$bc22569$[A] is asking often because connection feels uncertain, then each hesitant response makes the next request carry more weight. [B] wants less frequent together time and may pull back when every invitation feels like a verdict on commitment. The cycle makes [A] pursue harder and [B] protect space harder. Predictability can lower the temperature for both of you. Set one connection checkpoint that happens without negotiation, then let the time around it stay genuinely flexible.$bc22569$,$bc22570$Bring one realistic must-have to the agreement and stop using every open evening as a test of the relationship. Show up fully for the fixed checkpoint and give early notice when it needs to move, including a replacement time.$bc22570$,$bc22571$Case dismissed. Predictable closeness takes pressure off every invitation.$bc22571$,$bc22572$Approved v4 template$bc22572$,$bc22573$Root Swap$bc22573$,$bc22574$[A] = wants quality time more frequently; [B] = wants it less frequently.$bc22574$,$bc22575$Bring one realistic must-have to the agreement and stop using every open evening as a test of the relationship.$bc22575$,$bc22576$Show up fully for the fixed checkpoint and give early notice when it needs to move, including a replacement time.$bc22576$,$bc22577$Both of you save a three-week rhythm trial with one fixed connection checkpoint, one optional invitation window, protected solo time, and a dated review.$bc22577$,$bc22578$Case dismissed. Predictable closeness takes pressure off every invitation.$bc22578$),
